<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  AppxManifest.xml - Spaceadom's Microsoft Store (MSIX) package manifest.
  PROBLEM 250.

  THIS FILE IS A TEMPLATE. The four {{DOUBLE-BRACE}} placeholders are filled in
  by scripts/build-msix.ps1 from src-tauri/msix/identity.json (gitignored; see
  identity.example.json) and from package.json's version. It is still valid XML
  with the placeholders in place, on purpose, so that `[xml]` can load and
  structurally check it without a build having run.

  WHY THIS IS HAND-WRITTEN. Tauri v2 has no MSIX bundle target - the tracking
  issue (tauri-apps/tauri#4818, open since Aug 2022) is still open, and Tauri's
  own Store guidance is Route A: host a signed .exe and point the listing at it.
  The one third-party tool that does Tauri -> MSIX
  (@choochmeque/tauri-windows-bundle) was evaluated on 2026-09-05 and NOT
  adopted: it still requires the identity values, the assets, the Windows SDK
  and the certificate from you, so it removes none of the surface that actually
  costs anything, while adding a single-maintainer npm package with a bundled
  native binary to a project whose rule is explicit, auditable scripts. This
  file plus MakeAppx.exe is the whole of the alternative.

  ============================================================================
  WHAT IS DELIBERATELY *NOT* IN HERE
  ============================================================================

  * `desktop6:FileSystemWriteVirtualization` / `desktop6:RegistryWriteVirtualization`.
    The brief that produced this file asked about them. They are the opt-OUT
    switches for VIRTUALIZED (appContainer) packages; Microsoft's
    desktop-to-uwp-behind-the-scenes page scopes every AppData/registry
    redirection rule with "this section applies only to virtualized apps", and
    this package is `runFullTrust` (mediumIL), which is not one. Declaring an
    element that does not apply - together with the `unvirtualizedResources`
    restricted capability it needs - would be a Store-review question we could
    not answer and a manifest nobody could reconstruct the reason for later.
    The consequence for the app is in src/packaged.rs::migrate_legacy_data_once,
    which is written to be correct whether or not AppData turns out to be
    virtualized here. NOTE, honestly: no Microsoft page states in one sentence
    that a full-trust package is never virtualized. That is an inference.

  * A bundled WebView2 runtime, and any `win32dependencies:ExternalDependency`
    on it. Reasons, in order:
      - You cannot run an installer from inside a package, so `npm run store`'s
        offline-bootstrapper NSIS build has nothing to contribute here. The MSIX
        carries the app's FILES, not an installer. It is laid out from the plain
        `npm run tauri build` release binary.
      - The Evergreen WebView2 Runtime is a component of Windows 11 and is
        present on all but a small number of Windows 10 devices (Microsoft's own
        distribution page says both of those things).
      - `ExternalDependency` is honoured only by the App Installer app, not by
        Store-driven installs, so it would do nothing for the case we are
        shipping into and MakeAppx does not validate it either.
      - The remaining option is the Fixed Version runtime, +~250 MB inside the
        package. That is an OWNER DECISION, not a default: see
        to-publish-in-microsoft-store/SUBMIT-CHECKLIST.md, "Route B".
    RESIDUAL RISK, stated plainly: on a Windows 10 machine with no Evergreen
    runtime this package starts and shows no window. That is the same failure
    the app already has in that situation, and it is the reason Route A's
    installer embeds the offline runtime while this one cannot.

  * `resources.pri` and `.scale-200` asset variants. Without a `makepri.exe`
    step, files named `Logo.scale-200.png` are inert - Windows would never look
    at them - so shipping them would be decoration that reads as correctness.
    Only 100%-scale assets are generated (build-msix.ps1 makes them from
    icons/icon.png). Windows scales them. If the Store listing ever wants
    crisper tiles, the fix is a makepri step, not more files.

  ============================================================================
  THE ONE CROSS-FILE CONTRACT
  ============================================================================

  desktop:StartupTask/@TaskId below MUST equal `packaged::STARTUP_TASK_ID` in
  src-tauri/src/packaged.rs. `StartupTask::GetAsync` fails with E_INVALIDARG for
  an id the manifest does not declare, and in a log that is indistinguishable
  from "the WinRT API is unavailable" - so a typo here becomes a Store-only
  autostart bug that reads as something else entirely.

  Enabled="true" is allowed for a desktop startupTask and means the user gets
  autostart from the first launch without the app having to ask. They can turn
  it off in Settings > Apps > Startup or Task Manager > Startup apps, and if
  they do, Windows will not let the app switch it back on - which is why the
  Settings row goes inert with an explanation instead of pretending.
-->
<Package
  xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/appx/manifest/foundation/windows10"
  xmlns:uap="http://schemas.microsoft.com/appx/manifest/uap/windows10"
  xmlns:desktop="http://schemas.microsoft.com/appx/manifest/desktop/windows10"
  xmlns:rescap="http://schemas.microsoft.com/appx/manifest/foundation/windows10/restrictedcapabilities"
  IgnorableNamespaces="uap desktop rescap">

  <!-- All three filled from msix/identity.json; Version is a.b.c.0 from
       package.json, the fourth field left at 0 because the Store reserves it. -->
  <Identity
    Name="{{IDENTITY_NAME}}"
    Publisher="{{IDENTITY_PUBLISHER}}"
    Version="{{VERSION}}"
    ProcessorArchitecture="{{ARCH}}" />

  <Properties>
    <DisplayName>Spaceadom</DisplayName>
    <PublisherDisplayName>{{PUBLISHER_DISPLAY_NAME}}</PublisherDisplayName>
    <Logo>Assets\StoreLogo.png</Logo>
  </Properties>

  <Dependencies>
    <!-- MinVersion 10.0.17763.0 = Windows 10 1809. Two things set this floor:
         the windows.startupTask desktop extension (1607 and up) and WebView2,
         which is not realistically present below 1809. MaxVersionTested is the
         SDK this package is actually built with, 10.0.26100.0 - not a guess:
         it is the version of MakeAppx.exe that packs it. -->
    <TargetDeviceFamily Name="Windows.Universal"
                        MinVersion="10.0.17763.0"
                        MaxVersionTested="10.0.26100.0" />
  </Dependencies>

  <Resources>
    <Resource Language="en-us" />
  </Resources>

  <Applications>
    <!-- EntryPoint="Windows.FullTrustApplication" + Executable is the documented
         pair for a packaged classic Win32 app. It is what lets spaceadom.exe be
         an ordinary desktop process - which it must be, because a
         WH_KEYBOARD_LL hook is not something an appContainer app can install. -->
    <Application Id="Spaceadom"
                 Executable="spaceadom.exe"
                 EntryPoint="Windows.FullTrustApplication">

      <uap:VisualElements
        DisplayName="Spaceadom"
        Description="Hold Space and tap a key to launch, focus or minimise any app."
        BackgroundColor="transparent"
        Square150x150Logo="Assets\Square150x150Logo.png"
        Square44x44Logo="Assets\Square44x44Logo.png">
        <uap:DefaultTile
          Wide310x150Logo="Assets\Wide310x150Logo.png"
          Square71x71Logo="Assets\Square71x71Logo.png"
          Square310x310Logo="Assets\Square310x310Logo.png"
          ShortName="Spaceadom" />
        <!-- A tray utility has nothing to say on the lock screen and asks for
             no notification surface it does not use. -->
      </uap:VisualElements>

      <Extensions>
        <!-- PROBLEM 250 - autostart, the ONLY way that works in a package.
             TaskId must match packaged::STARTUP_TASK_ID (see the header). -->
        <desktop:Extension
          Category="windows.startupTask"
          Executable="spaceadom.exe"
          EntryPoint="Windows.FullTrustApplication">
          <desktop:StartupTask
            TaskId="SpaceadomStartupTask"
            Enabled="true"
            DisplayName="Spaceadom" />
        </desktop:Extension>
      </Extensions>

    </Application>
  </Applications>

  <Capabilities>
    <!-- The only capability this app needs, and the one that makes it a normal
         desktop process. The global keyboard hook, the tray icon, launching
         other applications and reading their window titles are all ordinary
         Win32 under full trust - there is no narrower capability that grants
         them, and no broader one is being asked for.

         `runFullTrust` is a RESTRICTED capability: it must be declared in the
         rescap namespace, and Partner Center asks you to justify it during
         submission. The justification is in LISTING.md's certification notes,
         and it is the same sentence the app has always given: the feature IS
         the spacebar behaving differently in other applications, which cannot
         be done without a system-wide hook. -->
    <rescap:Capability Name="runFullTrust" />
  </Capabilities>

</Package>
